#!/bin/bash
# First-boot script for the k3s node (skeleton).
#
# cloud-init runs this ONCE, as root, the first time the instance boots.
# Terraform renders it with templatefile(), so:
#
#   ${k3s_version}             is replaced by Terraform BEFORE the instance sees it
#   $${SOME_SHELL_VARIABLE}    two dollar signs: Terraform leaves a single
#                              dollar sign, and bash expands it at boot
#
# Getting those two mixed up is the classic templatefile mistake. Terraform
# fails with "Invalid reference" when it finds a single-dollar brace that is
# not one of the variables you passed in.
#
# Debugging: this script's output goes to /var/log/cloud-init-output.log on
# the instance. If k3s is not there after boot, read that file first:
#   aws ssm start-session --target <instance-id>
#   sudo tail -n 50 /var/log/cloud-init-output.log

# Stop at the first failing command, treat unset variables as errors, and
# print each command. A half-configured node that looks healthy is worse than
# one that failed loudly.
set -euxo pipefail

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TODO(3.4) step 1: cost safety net (dead-man switch).
#
# Schedule the machine to power itself off ${auto_shutdown_minutes} minutes
# from now, FIRST, before anything that could fail. If you forget
# `terraform destroy`, close the laptop, or the script below breaks, the
# instance still stops costing credits.
# One line with the standard `shutdown` command does it.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TODO(3.4) step 2: install k3s, pinned to ${k3s_version}.
#
# Use the official install script (https://get.k3s.io) and control it with
# environment variables and flags rather than editing anything by hand:
#
#   INSTALL_K3S_VERSION    pins the release; without it every rebuild installs
#                          whatever is newest that day
#   --disable traefik      k3s bundles the Traefik ingress controller, which
#                          claims ports 80 and 443 on the node. The MUST path
#                          exposes the app with a LoadBalancer Service through
#                          k3s's built-in ServiceLB, which needs port 80 free.
#                          (Re-enabling Traefik with the Gateway API is a
#                          SHOULD item in Week 4.)
#
# Read https://docs.k3s.io/installation/configuration before writing the line.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TODO(3.4) step 3: wait until the node reports Ready.
#
# The install script returns before the cluster is usable. Poll with the
# bundled `k3s kubectl` and give up with a non-zero exit after a few minutes,
# so a broken boot is visible in the log instead of looking finished.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TODO(3.4) step 4: install Helm ${helm_version}.
#
# The deploy pipeline runs `helm upgrade` ON this machine through Systems
# Manager, so Helm must be here. Use the official installer script for Helm 4
# from the helm/helm repository and pin the version (it reads DESIRED_VERSION).
#
# Helm needs to know where the cluster is. k3s writes its kubeconfig to
# /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml, readable by root only. Leave those permissions
# alone; Run Command executes as root. Make KUBECONFIG point at that file for
# root's commands.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TODO(4.4) step 5 (leave this until Week 4): namespace and database password.
#
# The chart refers to a Kubernetes Secret by name and never contains the
# password. Something has to create that Secret on a brand-new node, or the
# Week 5 "rebuild from code" drill needs a manual step.
#
# Create the namespace, then create the Secret with a password GENERATED HERE
# at boot (for example with `openssl rand -hex 24`), piped straight into
# `k3s kubectl create secret generic ... --from-literal=...`.
#
# Why this is safe when "a secret in user data" normally is not: user data is
# readable by anyone who can call ec2:DescribeInstanceAttribute, so a literal
# password here would be exposed. A COMMAND that generates a password is not
# a password. The value exists only inside the cluster, never in git, never
# in Terraform state, never in a pipeline log.
#
# Careful with `set -x` above: it prints every command, with its arguments,
# into /var/log/cloud-init-output.log. Switch tracing off around this step
# (`set +x` ... `set -x`) or the generated password lands in that log.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TODO(3.4) step 6: leave a marker so you can tell the script finished, for
# example a file under /var/lib/ with the versions that were installed.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
